/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cloud-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("cloud-plus", [["cp1",0,1,"A development team wants to deploy its web application code without patching operating systems or managing the language runtime, but it still wants to control the application itself. Which service model fits?",["Platform as a service","Infrastructure as a service with a hardened image","Software as a service","Colocation in a provider's data center"],0,"PaaS runs the OS and runtime for you while you deploy and manage your own code. IaaS leaves OS patching to the customer, and SaaS gives you someone else's finished application.","Domain 1: service models"],["cp2",0,1,"A company runs Linux VMs on a public IaaS platform. An auditor asks who is responsible for applying security patches to the guest operating system. What is the correct answer?",["The cloud provider, as part of the hypervisor maintenance","The provider for critical patches and the customer for the rest","The customer","Whoever built the marketplace image the VM came from"],2,"Under the shared responsibility model for IaaS, the provider secures hardware and the hypervisor, and everything from the guest OS up is the customer's job. Choosing a marketplace image does not shift that duty.","Domain 1: shared responsibility"],["cp3",0,1,"A hospital keeps its patient database in its own data center for regulatory reasons but wants its public website to scale into a public cloud during flu season. Which deployment model describes this?",["Community cloud","Private cloud","Multicloud with two SaaS vendors","Hybrid cloud"],3,"Hybrid cloud combines on-premises or private infrastructure with public cloud and connects them. Multicloud means using several public providers, which is not what is described.","Domain 1: deployment models"],["cp4",0,1,"Several state agencies with the same compliance requirements want to share one cloud environment that is not open to the general public. Which deployment model is this?",["Private cloud for each agency","Public cloud","Hybrid cloud","Community cloud"],3,"A community cloud is shared by organizations with common requirements such as regulation or mission. Giving each agency its own private cloud would not be shared.","Domain 1: deployment models"],["cp5",0,1,"An application must keep running if one data center fails, and its database needs synchronous replication with very low latency. Where should the architect place the instances?",["In multiple availability zones within one region","In one availability zone on separate hosts","In two regions on different continents","On one dedicated host with redundant power supplies"],0,"Availability zones in a region are separate facilities joined by low-latency links, so synchronous replication works and a single data center failure is survived. Distant regions add too much latency for synchronous writes.","Domain 1: regions and zones"],["cp6",0,1,"A licensing agreement requires that a vendor's software run on physical servers that are not shared with any other cloud customer. Which option meets this?",["Spot instances in a private subnet","Burstable instances with CPU credits","Dedicated hosts","A larger general-purpose instance size"],2,"Dedicated hosts give you a physical server used only by your account, which satisfies per-socket or per-core licensing and isolation rules. Instance size or network placement does not remove multitenancy.","Domain 1: compute and multitenancy"],["cp7",0,1,"For disaster recovery, a company keeps its core database continuously replicated to a second region, while application servers there remain as images that are launched only when a disaster is declared. Which DR approach is this?",["Warm standby","Backup and restore","Multisite active-active","Pilot light"],3,"Pilot light keeps only the critical core (usually data) running and live, and starts the rest on demand. Warm standby keeps a scaled-down but running copy of the full stack.","Domain 1: disaster recovery"],["cp8",0,1,"Twenty Linux web servers must all read and write the same set of uploaded documents through a standard mounted file system. Which storage type should be provisioned?",["Block storage volume attached to each server","Managed file storage accessed over NFS","Archive object storage","A local instance store on each server"],1,"Managed file storage provides a shared file system that many servers mount at once over NFS or SMB. A block volume normally attaches to one instance, and archive storage is for rarely read data.","Domain 1: storage types"],["cp9",0,1,"A media company needs to store billions of images, served to apps through HTTPS calls, with virtually unlimited capacity and pay-per-GB pricing. Which storage type is the best fit?",["Block storage","Object storage","File storage","Instance store volumes"],1,"Object storage scales almost without limit, is accessed through HTTP APIs and bills per GB stored. Block and file storage are provisioned for servers and do not suit billions of web-served objects.","Domain 1: storage types"],["cp10",0,1,"A transactional database on a cloud VM needs consistent low-latency disk performance with a guaranteed number of I/O operations per second. What should be provisioned?",["A block volume with provisioned IOPS","An object storage bucket mounted with a FUSE driver","A cold file share tier","An archive storage tier with expedited retrieval"],0,"Block volumes with provisioned IOPS give predictable latency and throughput for databases. Object storage mounted through a driver and cold or archive tiers are far too slow for transactional I/O.","Domain 1: storage performance"],["cp11",0,1,"Audit logs must be kept for seven years and are almost never read; retrieving them within 12 hours is acceptable. Which storage choice minimizes cost?",["Archive object storage tier","Provisioned IOPS block volumes","Standard object storage tier","Premium file share"],0,"Archive tiers have the lowest storage price in exchange for slow, paid retrieval, which suits long retention with rare access. Standard tier costs more per GB, and block or premium file storage is far more expensive.","Domain 1: storage tiers"],["cp12",0,1,"Each time a customer uploads a receipt image, a small piece of code must create a thumbnail. Uploads are unpredictable, and the team wants to pay nothing when idle. Which approach fits?",["A reserved VM that polls the bucket every minute","A function triggered by the storage upload event","A container cluster with three always-on nodes","A scheduled nightly batch job on a spot instance"],1,"Serverless functions run in response to events and bill per invocation, so idle time costs nothing. Always-on VMs or nodes cost money while idle, and a nightly batch delays the thumbnail.","Domain 1: serverless"],["cp13",0,1,"Which statement correctly describes how containers differ from virtual machines?",["Containers share the host operating system kernel, while each VM runs its own guest OS","Containers each include a full guest OS and a hypervisor","Containers cannot run on cloud infrastructure, only on physical servers","Containers provide stronger isolation than VMs because they use hardware virtualization"],0,"Containers package an application and its dependencies but share the host kernel, making them lighter and faster to start. VMs virtualize hardware and each runs a complete OS, which gives stronger isolation.","Domain 1: containers"],["cp14",0,1,"An online store's checkout service is overloaded during sales, while its catalog and review services are idle. The team splits the monolith into separate services. Which benefit does this give most directly?",["Fewer network calls between components","A single shared database schema for all features","Each service can be scaled independently","Simpler end-to-end debugging"],2,"Microservices let each component scale, deploy and fail on its own, so checkout can scale without scaling everything. They usually increase network calls and make debugging harder, not simpler.","Domain 1: microservices"],["cp15",0,1,"Instances in a private subnet must download OS updates from the internet but must never accept inbound connections from it. What should be added?",["A NAT gateway in a public subnet, with a default route from the private subnet","Public IP addresses on each instance","An internet gateway route directly from the private subnet","A VPC peering connection to another private network"],0,"A NAT gateway lets private instances start outbound connections while blocking unsolicited inbound ones. Routing the private subnet straight to an internet gateway or adding public IPs would expose the instances.","Domain 1: virtual networks"],["cp16",0,1,"A company moves large nightly data sets between its data center and the cloud and needs consistent latency and bandwidth that do not travel over the public internet. Which connection should it use?",["A site-to-site IPsec VPN over broadband","A dedicated private interconnect such as Direct Connect, ExpressRoute or Cloud Interconnect","A client VPN on the backup server","A public endpoint protected by TLS"],1,"Dedicated interconnects are private circuits into the provider with predictable performance. A site-to-site VPN is encrypted but still rides the internet, so latency and throughput vary.","Domain 1: hybrid connectivity"],["cp17",0,1,"A web application needs requests for /api/* sent to one group of servers and /images/* to another, based on the URL path. Which component provides this?",["A network (layer 4) load balancer","DNS round robin across both server groups","An application (layer 7) load balancer","A NAT gateway"],2,"Layer 7 load balancers read HTTP details such as path and host header and route accordingly. Layer 4 balancers only see IP addresses and ports, and DNS round robin cannot inspect paths.","Domain 1: load balancing"],["cp18",0,1,"Users in Asia complain that a site hosted in a European region loads its images and scripts slowly. The content rarely changes. What is the most cost-effective improvement?",["Move the whole application to a larger, compute-optimized instance size","Add a second database read replica in Europe","Serve static content through a content delivery network","Increase the load balancer idle timeout"],2,"A CDN caches static files at edge locations near users, cutting latency. A bigger instance or more replicas in Europe does nothing for the distance to Asia.","Domain 1: content delivery"],["cp19",0,1,"A company's ERP servers run 24 hours a day at steady load and will stay in the cloud for at least three years. Which pricing model gives the largest savings?",["Reserved instances or committed use discounts","On-demand instances","Spot instances","Pay-per-invocation pricing by rewriting the ERP as serverless functions"],0,"Committing to one or three years of steady usage earns large discounts. Spot capacity can be reclaimed, which is not acceptable for an ERP, and on-demand is the most expensive for constant use.","Domain 1: cost models"],["cp20",0,1,"A research lab runs thousands of simulation jobs that checkpoint their progress and can restart if interrupted. It wants the lowest compute cost. Which option fits?",["Spot or preemptible instances","Dedicated hosts","Three-year reserved instances","On-demand instances in two regions"],0,"Spot capacity is deeply discounted but can be reclaimed at short notice, which is fine for fault-tolerant, checkpointed batch work. Dedicated hosts and on-demand cost more, and reservations suit steady, always-on load.","Domain 1: cost models"],["cp21",0,1,"Finance wants each department's monthly cloud spend reported back to it without actually moving money between cost centers. What should be implemented?",["Showback reports built on consistent resource tags such as department and project","Chargeback using separate credit cards per team","A single shared account with no tags","Reserved instances purchased by each department"],0,"Showback reports costs to teams for awareness, and consistent tags make the costs attributable. Chargeback actually bills the departments, which finance said it does not want.","Domain 1: cost management"],["cp22",0,2,"A team runs two identical production environments. The new release is deployed to the idle one, tested, and then all traffic is switched to it at the load balancer, with the old one kept for instant rollback. Which strategy is this?",["Canary","Rolling","Blue-green","In-place upgrade of each server"],2,"Blue-green keeps two full environments and switches traffic between them, so rollback means switching back. Canary moves a small percentage of users first, and rolling updates instances in batches.","Domain 2: deployment strategies"],["cp23",0,2,"A company releases a new version to 5% of users, watches error rates and latency for an hour, and then gradually increases the share if the metrics stay healthy. Which strategy is this?",["Blue-green","Canary","Big bang","Recreate"],1,"A canary release exposes a small slice of real traffic first and widens it as confidence grows. Blue-green switches all traffic at once.","Domain 2: deployment strategies"],["cp24",0,2,"An autoscaling group of 12 web servers must be updated without a second full environment and without dropping below 75% capacity. Which approach fits?",["Blue-green deployment","Terminate all instances and relaunch with the new image","A canary release to a separate region","A rolling deployment in batches of three instances"],3,"Rolling updates replace instances a batch at a time, so replacing three of twelve keeps 75% serving. Blue-green would need a second full environment, and terminating everything causes downtime.","Domain 2: deployment strategies"],["cp25",0,2,"A data center lease ends in four months. The company must move 200 VMs to the cloud as quickly as possible, with no code changes, and optimize later. Which migration strategy fits?",["Refactor into microservices","Rehost (lift and shift)","Repurchase as SaaS","Retire all 200 servers"],1,"Rehosting moves VMs largely as they are, which is fastest under a deadline. Refactoring takes much longer, and repurchasing or retiring only fits some applications.","Domain 2: migration strategies"],["cp26",0,2,"During migration, a team moves its self-managed SQL Server VM to the provider's managed SQL database service, with only connection-string changes to the application. Which strategy is this?",["Rehost","Replatform","Retain","Refactor (re-architect)"],1,"Replatforming makes small optimizations, such as adopting a managed database, without redesigning the application. Refactoring would change the application architecture itself.","Domain 2: migration strategies"],["cp27",0,2,"A company decides to stop running its on-premises customer relationship software and moves its users to a subscription CRM service instead. Which migration strategy is this?",["Replatform","Retain","Rehost","Repurchase"],3,"Repurchasing replaces an application with a different product, usually SaaS. Rehosting and replatforming move the same application.","Domain 2: migration strategies"],["cp28",0,2,"A company must move 400 TB of archive data to the cloud over a 200 Mbps internet link that is already busy. Which approach is most practical?",["Order an offline data transfer appliance from the provider","Use a site-to-site VPN to improve throughput","Compress the data and upload it over the existing link","Copy the data with many parallel HTTPS upload streams from several servers"],0,"At 200 Mbps, 400 TB would take more than half a year even with the link fully free, so shipping a transfer appliance is faster. A VPN adds encryption, not bandwidth, and parallel uploads cannot exceed the link.","Domain 2: data transfer"],["cp29",0,2,"Before a migration, the team uses a discovery tool and finds that a reporting server makes nightly calls to the payroll database. Why does this matter for planning?",["The dependent servers should be moved in the same migration wave or connectivity must be planned","It means the payroll database must be retired","It proves the reporting server should be rehosted as SaaS","It shows the network needs larger subnets"],0,"Dependency mapping groups tightly linked systems into the same wave, or ensures hybrid connectivity during the move, so nothing breaks at cutover.","Domain 2: migration planning"],["cp30",0,2,"New web servers take 40 minutes to become ready because each one installs packages and agents at boot. The team wants them ready in a few minutes with identical settings. What should it use?",["A longer autoscaling cooldown period","A pre-built golden image with the software already installed","Manual configuration of each new server by the operations team using a written checklist","A larger instance type"],1,"A golden image bakes in software, agents and hardening, so instances boot ready and consistent. A bigger instance or longer cooldown does not remove the install time.","Domain 2: images and templates"],["cp31",0,2,"An in-memory caching cluster keeps running out of RAM while its CPUs sit at 10%. Which instance family should be chosen when it is redeployed?",["Memory-optimized","Compute-optimized","Storage-optimized","GPU-accelerated"],0,"Memory-optimized families offer a high RAM-to-vCPU ratio, which suits caches and in-memory databases. Compute-optimized would add CPU the workload does not need.","Domain 2: provisioning compute"],["cp32",0,2,"A private cloud administrator allocated 40 TB of thin-provisioned volumes on a 20 TB storage pool. What is the main risk to monitor?",["Volumes will be slower because space is pre-zeroed","Thin provisioning disables encryption at rest","Snapshots cannot be taken of thin volumes","The pool can fill up as volumes grow, causing write failures for every volume on it"],3,"Thin provisioning allocates space on write, so overcommitting is allowed, but if real usage reaches the pool's capacity, writes fail. Pre-zeroing is a thick-provisioning behavior.","Domain 2: provisioning storage"],["cp33",0,2,"A managed relational database is overloaded by reporting queries, while writes from the main application are light. Which change best reduces the load on the primary?",["Enable a multi-AZ standby replica and point the reporting tool at the standby's endpoint","Increase the backup retention period","Switch the database to an archive storage tier","Create a read replica and send reporting queries to it"],3,"Read replicas serve read-only traffic such as reports, taking load off the primary. In many services the multi-AZ standby only takes over on failover and cannot serve reads.","Domain 2: managed services"],["cp34",0,2,"A team defines its network, VMs and database in version-controlled templates and deploys identical development, staging and production environments from them. What is the main benefit?",["It removes the need for any testing","It guarantees the cloud bill will fall","Environments are repeatable and consistent, with changes reviewed in code","It replaces the need for monitoring"],2,"Infrastructure as code makes environments repeatable and reviewable, reducing configuration drift. It does not remove the need for testing or monitoring, and it does not by itself cut costs.","Domain 2: infrastructure as code"],["cp35",0,2,"A company's policy says running servers are never patched or changed by hand; instead, a new image is built and the servers are replaced. What is this practice called?",["Configuration drift","In-place upgrading with a configuration tool","Immutable infrastructure","Vertical scaling"],2,"Immutable infrastructure replaces servers with new ones built from updated images rather than modifying them. In-place upgrades change running systems, and drift is what this practice prevents.","Domain 2: immutable infrastructure"],["cp36",0,2,"Right after a production release, the team runs a short automated script that signs in, adds an item to the cart and loads the order page. What is this test called?",["Load test","Smoke test","Penetration test","User acceptance test"],1,"A smoke test quickly checks that the most important functions work after deployment. Load tests measure capacity, penetration tests look for vulnerabilities, and UAT is business sign-off before release.","Domain 2: post-deployment validation"],["cp37",0,2,"A team wants to run containers across several nodes, with failed containers restarted automatically and replicas spread across zones, but without managing the control plane. What should it deploy?",["A managed Kubernetes service","A single large VM running Docker","A serverless function per container","A file share for container images"],0,"Managed Kubernetes provides scheduling, self-healing and replica management while the provider runs the control plane. A single VM has no cross-node scheduling or zone spread.","Domain 2: managed services"],["cp38",0,2,"A migration cutover is complete. Which step should come next before the old environment is shut down?",["Validate functionality and performance against the pre-migration baseline","Delete all backups from the source system","Disable monitoring to avoid false alerts","Remove DNS records for the new environment"],0,"Post-migration validation compares the new system with the baseline and confirms it works before decommissioning the source. Deleting backups or disabling monitoring removes your safety net.","Domain 2: migration validation"],["cp39",0,3,"A stateless web tier behind a load balancer slows down during traffic spikes. The team wants to add capacity without any downtime. What should it do?",["Scale vertically by resizing each instance to the next larger size one at a time","Move the web tier to an archive storage tier","Scale horizontally by adding instances through autoscaling","Increase the DNS TTL"],2,"Horizontal scaling adds instances behind the load balancer without interrupting service, which suits stateless tiers. Resizing an instance usually needs a restart.","Domain 3: scaling"],["cp40",0,3,"A legacy application cannot run on more than one server, and it is now CPU-bound. Which scaling option is available?",["Horizontal scaling with an autoscaling group","Adding a content delivery network","Adding read replicas to the web tier","Vertical scaling to a larger instance size during a maintenance window"],3,"If the app cannot be distributed, the only option is to scale up to a bigger instance, which usually needs a restart. Horizontal scaling requires the app to run on several servers.","Domain 3: scaling"],["cp41",0,3,"A payroll system's load rises sharply every Monday at 8:00, and reactive scaling adds servers too late. Which autoscaling approach fixes this?",["Removing the load balancer health check","A longer cooldown on the CPU policy","A higher CPU threshold for scaling out, so that brief spikes are ignored","Scheduled scaling that adds capacity shortly before 8:00 on Mondays"],3,"Scheduled scaling prepares capacity ahead of a known, predictable peak. A longer cooldown or higher threshold would make the reaction even slower.","Domain 3: autoscaling"],["cp42",0,3,"Users report that some checkout requests take eight seconds, and a request passes through six microservices. Which tool best shows which service is adding the delay?",["CPU metrics for each host","Distributed tracing","A daily log archive","An uptime check on the home page"],1,"Distributed tracing follows a single request across services and shows the time spent in each. Host CPU metrics and uptime checks cannot attribute latency to a specific hop.","Domain 3: observability"],["cp43",0,3,"The on-call engineer receives hundreds of CPU alerts a week, and most clear on their own within a minute. What is the best improvement?",["Tune thresholds against a baseline and require the condition to persist for several minutes","Send every alert to the whole team instead","Disable CPU alerting entirely","Lower the CPU threshold to catch problems earlier"],0,"Alert fatigue is reduced by using baselines and a sustained duration, so only meaningful conditions page someone. Disabling alerts hides real problems, and lowering the threshold creates even more noise.","Domain 3: alerting"],["cp44",0,3,"Security requires that logs from 300 VMs and several managed services be searchable in one place and kept for one year, even if an instance is deleted. What should be implemented?",["Local log rotation on each VM with a one-year rotation schedule configured","Screenshots of dashboards stored weekly","Centralized log aggregation with a retention policy","Larger boot disks on each VM"],2,"Shipping logs to a central service keeps them searchable and retained independently of the source instance. Local logs vanish when the VM is deleted.","Domain 3: logging"],["cp45",0,3,"A company takes a full backup every Sunday and differential backups every other night. The server fails on Thursday afternoon. Which backups are needed to restore it to Wednesday night's state?",["Sunday's full and every differential from Monday to Wednesday","Only Wednesday's differential","Sunday's full and Wednesday's differential","Every full backup from the last month"],2,"A differential holds all changes since the last full backup, so you need the full plus the latest differential. Needing every nightly backup describes an incremental scheme.","Domain 3: backup types"],["cp46",0,3,"After ransomware hit a cloud account, the attacker also deleted every snapshot stored in that same account. Which change best prevents this next time?",["Take snapshots more frequently in the same account","Store backups in a separate account with immutable retention and restricted delete rights","Encrypt snapshots with the provider-managed key","Keep only one snapshot to reduce cost"],1,"Isolated, immutable backups in a separate security boundary cannot be deleted by an attacker who compromises the production account. More frequent snapshots in the same account are just as exposed.","Domain 3: backup protection"],["cp47",0,3,"The backup job has reported success every night for a year, but no one has ever restored from it. What is the most important next step?",["Perform a scheduled test restore and verify the data and the recovery time","Switch the backup schedule to weekly","Increase the retention period to five years","Move the backups to a cheaper archive tier and extend their retention to cover a full audit cycle"],0,"Only a restore test proves backups are usable and shows whether the RTO can be met. A successful job status does not confirm the data can actually be recovered.","Domain 3: restore testing"],["cp48",0,3,"A company must apply a critical OS patch to 50 web servers behind a load balancer without causing an outage. What is the best approach?",["Patch all servers at once during peak hours","Patch only the servers that report errors","Wait for the next major OS release instead","Patch in small batches, removing each batch from the load balancer, then verify before continuing"],3,"Patching in batches while the rest keep serving traffic avoids downtime, and verifying between batches limits the impact of a bad patch.","Domain 3: patch management"],["cp49",0,3,"The provider announces that the managed database engine version a company uses will reach end of support in six months and will then be upgraded automatically. What should the team do?",["Ignore it, since keeping managed services on supported versions is entirely the provider's responsibility","Disable automatic backups to block the upgrade","Plan, test and schedule the upgrade in a maintenance window before the deadline","Move the database to a larger instance to avoid the upgrade"],2,"Deprecations should be handled proactively: test the application on the new version and upgrade on your schedule rather than accept an unplanned forced upgrade. Instance size has no effect on version support.","Domain 3: lifecycle management"],["cp50",0,3,"Monitoring shows an 8-vCPU application server has averaged 4% CPU and 15% memory use for three months. What should the administrator recommend?",["Enable vertical autoscaling to a larger size","Add a second identical server for redundancy of capacity","Buy a three-year reservation for the current size","Right-size it to a smaller instance type"],3,"Consistently low utilization means the instance is oversized; right-sizing cuts cost. Reserving the oversized instance would lock in the waste.","Domain 3: right-sizing"],["cp51",0,3,"A service has a 99.9% monthly availability SLA. Roughly how much downtime does this allow in a 30-day month?",["About 4.3 minutes","About 7 hours","About 1 minute","About 43 minutes"],3,"0.1% of 43,200 minutes is about 43 minutes. About 4 minutes corresponds to 99.99%.","Domain 3: SLAs"],["cp52",0,3,"Objects in a log bucket are read often for the first 30 days, occasionally for a year and then never, but must be kept for seven years. What should be configured?",["A larger standard-tier bucket","Manual monthly moves between tiers by an administrator, followed by manual deletion once the logs are seven years old","Versioning with no expiration","A lifecycle policy that moves objects to cooler tiers over time and deletes them after seven years"],3,"Lifecycle policies automatically transition objects to cheaper tiers as access drops and expire them at the end of retention. Manual moves are error-prone, and versioning alone increases storage.","Domain 3: storage lifecycle"],["cp53",0,3,"A company wants to know that its sign-in page works for users in three continents before customers report a problem. What should it set up?",["Host-level disk metrics","A monthly penetration test of the sign-in page by an external security firm","Synthetic monitoring that runs scripted sign-ins from several locations","A longer log retention period"],2,"Synthetic monitors run scripted user journeys from different regions on a schedule and alert when they fail. Disk metrics and log retention do not test the user experience.","Domain 3: observability"],["cp54",0,4,"An application on a cloud VM needs to read files from an object storage bucket. A developer proposes putting an access key in the application's configuration file. What is the better approach?",["Attach an instance role or managed identity with read-only permission to that bucket","Commit the key to a private repository","Store the key in an environment variable on the VM and rotate it by hand every ninety days","Make the bucket public and restrict it by obscurity"],0,"Workload identities give the VM short-lived credentials automatically, with least-privilege access and no stored secrets. Environment variables and repositories still hold long-lived keys that can leak.","Domain 4: workload identity"],["cp55",0,4,"A new cloud account was opened with the root (owner) user, which the team still uses for everyday tasks. What should be done first?",["Enable MFA on the root user, create named admin accounts for daily work and lock the root credentials away","Share the root password with the whole team for convenience","Delete the root user","Create access keys for the root user for automation"],0,"The root user should be protected with MFA and used only for the few tasks that require it. It generally cannot be deleted, and root access keys are a major risk.","Domain 4: identity"],["cp56",0,4,"A company wants employees to sign in to the cloud console with their existing corporate directory accounts, so that disabling an employee in the directory removes cloud access. What should be configured?",["Separate cloud user accounts whose passwords are kept in sync with the directory by a nightly script","Federation with the corporate identity provider using SAML or OpenID Connect","A shared administrator account with MFA","Access keys for every employee"],1,"Federation lets the corporate IdP authenticate users, so access is removed centrally when the account is disabled. Separate cloud accounts must be removed by hand and easily get missed.","Domain 4: federation and SSO"],["cp57",0,4,"A regulator requires that the company control, rotate and be able to disable the keys that encrypt its stored data, with audit logs of every key use. What should be used?",["Provider-managed default encryption keys, which are turned on automatically for all storage","Customer-managed keys in the provider's key management service","TLS 1.3 on all endpoints","Hashing all stored files"],1,"Customer-managed keys in a KMS give control over key policy, rotation and disabling, with usage logged. Default provider keys give less control, TLS protects data in transit and hashing is not reversible encryption.","Domain 4: key management"],["cp58",0,4,"A web server's security group allows inbound TCP 443 from anywhere. The subnet's network ACL allows inbound 443 but has no outbound rules beyond the default deny. Clients cannot load the site. Why?",["Security groups are stateless, so they need an outbound rule","Network ACLs are stateless, so return traffic to client ephemeral ports needs an outbound allow rule","The load balancer must use port 80","TLS certificates cannot be used behind a network ACL"],1,"NACLs do not track connections, so responses to ephemeral ports must be allowed explicitly. Security groups are stateful and permit return traffic automatically.","Domain 4: network security controls"],["cp59",0,4,"Logs show attackers sending requests containing ' OR 1=1 -- to a public web application's login form. Which control can block these requests before they reach the app?",["A network ACL rule that denies inbound port 443 from unknown sources","A NAT gateway","A web application firewall with SQL injection rules","A larger load balancer"],2,"A WAF inspects HTTP requests and blocks common attack patterns such as SQL injection. Blocking port 443 would stop all legitimate traffic too.","Domain 4: WAF"],["cp60",0,4,"A developer committed a database password to a Git repository. After rotating the password, how should the application get it in the future?",["Retrieve it at runtime from a secrets manager using the workload's identity","Hard-code it in the container image","Put it in the VM's user data script, which runs only once at first boot and is then hidden from users","Store it base64-encoded in the repository"],0,"A secrets manager stores, audits and rotates secrets, and the application fetches them at runtime with its own identity. Base64 is encoding, not encryption, and images and user data are easily read.","Domain 4: secrets management"],["cp61",0,4,"Privacy law requires that EU customer data never be stored outside the EU. How can a cloud administrator best enforce this?",["Ask developers to remember the rule","Encrypt data with TLS","Apply an organization-wide policy that denies resource creation outside approved EU regions, and check backup and replica locations","Use a CDN with global edge caching for all customer data so that EU users always get fast responses from the nearest edge location"],2,"A preventive policy blocks non-compliant regions, and backups, replicas and logs must also stay in approved locations. A global CDN could cache personal data outside the EU.","Domain 4: data sovereignty"],["cp62",0,4,"A team wants to stop container images with critical vulnerabilities from ever reaching production. Where should image scanning be added?",["Only in production, once a quarter","In the CI/CD pipeline, failing the build when critical findings exist, plus registry scanning","On developers' laptops only","After an incident is reported"],1,"Scanning in the pipeline stops vulnerable images before deployment, and registry scanning catches newly published CVEs in stored images. Quarterly or after-incident scans are too late.","Domain 4: vulnerability management"],["cp63",0,4,"Across 40 cloud accounts, the security team wants continuous detection of misconfigurations such as public storage buckets, open management ports and disabled logging. Which tool category fits?",["Configuration management database only","Endpoint antivirus","Cloud security posture management","A network tap"],2,"CSPM tools continuously evaluate cloud configurations against best practices and compliance rules. Antivirus runs on hosts and cannot see account-level misconfigurations.","Domain 4: CSPM"],["cp64",0,4,"An attacker who compromises one web server should not be able to reach the payment service on other ports. Every workload should allow only the flows it needs. Which approach does this describe?",["A single flat subnet with one broad security group","Microsegmentation with least-privilege rules between workloads","Placing all servers in a public subnet","Opening all ports internally and filtering only at the perimeter"],1,"Microsegmentation, a key zero trust practice, limits east-west movement by allowing only required flows between workloads. A flat network trusts everything inside the perimeter.","Domain 4: zero trust"],["cp65",0,4,"A cloud VM is sending traffic to a known command-and-control address. The security team wants to stop the activity while preserving evidence. What should it do first?",["Terminate the instance immediately and launch a clean replacement from the latest golden image","Reboot the instance to clear memory","Delete its logs to save storage","Move it to an isolation security group with no outbound access and snapshot its disks"],3,"Isolating the VM contains the threat while snapshots preserve disk evidence. Terminating or rebooting destroys volatile and possibly disk evidence.","Domain 4: incident response"],["cp66",0,4,"A production database was deleted overnight, and management wants to know which identity did it and from which IP address. Where should the administrator look?",["The CDN cache statistics","CPU metrics from the database","The provider's API audit log, such as CloudTrail, Azure Activity Log or Cloud Audit Logs","The VM's boot diagnostics"],2,"Control-plane audit logs record who called which API, when and from where. Performance metrics do not identify users.","Domain 4: audit logging"],["cp67",0,4,"A company wants to detect and block employees uploading files that contain credit card numbers to cloud storage shared outside the company. Which control fits?",["Data loss prevention with content inspection","Autoscaling policies on the file-sharing servers","A read replica","Reserved capacity"],0,"DLP classifies content, such as card number patterns, and can alert on or block sharing it. The other options have nothing to do with data protection.","Domain 4: data protection"],["cp68",0,4,"A new standard VM image must meet an industry-recognized secure configuration baseline before it is approved. Which reference is commonly used?",["An SLA from the provider","A CIS benchmark for that operating system","The provider's pricing calculator for that operating system","The OSI model"],1,"CIS benchmarks give detailed, consensus hardening settings for operating systems and cloud services. An SLA covers availability, not configuration.","Domain 4: hardening"],["cp69",0,4,"Card payment data is replaced in the application database with random surrogate values, and only a separate secured vault can map them back to real card numbers. What is this technique?",["Tokenization","Hashing","Compression","Salted hashing"],0,"Tokenization swaps sensitive data for tokens with no mathematical link to the original, and the mapping lives in a secured vault. Hashing is one-way and cannot be mapped back.","Domain 4: data protection"],["cp70",0,4,"A company wants its VMs to reach a managed storage service without any traffic crossing the public internet, and wants the service's public endpoint blocked. What should be configured?",["A CDN in front of the storage service, with the storage account's firewall set to allow all networks","A public IP on each VM","An internet gateway with a larger bandwidth limit","A private endpoint (private link) for the storage service in the virtual network"],3,"A private endpoint gives the service a private IP inside the virtual network, so traffic stays on the provider backbone and public access can be disabled.","Domain 4: network security controls"],["cp71",0,5,"Two engineers change the same Terraform module. The team wants every change reviewed and tested before it reaches the main branch. Which workflow fits?",["Everyone commits directly to main","Editing the files on a shared network drive","Emailing ZIP files of changes to a lead","Feature branches merged through pull requests with required review and passing checks"],3,"Feature branches and pull requests allow review and automated checks before merging. Committing straight to main skips both.","Domain 5: source control"],["cp72",0,5,"Every time code is pushed, a pipeline automatically compiles it, runs unit tests and produces a versioned artifact. Which practice is this?",["Configuration drift","Blue-green deployment strategy","Continuous integration","Chaos engineering"],2,"Continuous integration builds and tests every change automatically so problems are caught early. Blue-green is a release strategy, not a build practice.","Domain 5: CI/CD"],["cp73",0,5,"A pipeline automatically builds, tests and deploys every change to staging, but a release manager must click Approve before production. What does this describe?",["Continuous deployment","Continuous delivery","Manual deployment","Continuous integration only"],1,"Continuous delivery keeps software always releasable, with a manual gate to production. Continuous deployment would release to production automatically with no approval.","Domain 5: CI/CD"],["cp74",0,5,"An operations team wants to configure 200 Linux servers over SSH, without installing an agent on them, using YAML playbooks. Which tool fits?",["Puppet","Chef","CloudFormation","Ansible"],3,"Ansible is agentless, connects over SSH (or WinRM) and uses YAML playbooks. Puppet and Chef normally use agents, and CloudFormation provisions AWS resources rather than configuring servers.","Domain 5: configuration management"],["cp75",0,5,"An engineer writes a file that states 'there should be three web servers and one load balancer', and the tool works out whether to create, change or delete resources. What kind of approach is this?",["Imperative scripting with ordered steps","Declarative infrastructure as code","Manual provisioning","Ad hoc CLI commands"],1,"Declarative IaC describes the desired end state and the tool calculates the steps. Imperative scripts spell out each step in order.","Domain 5: infrastructure as code"],["cp76",0,5,"A team wants its pipeline to start automatically whenever code is pushed to the repository, instead of polling every five minutes. What should be configured?",["A webhook from the repository to the pipeline service","A cron job on the build server that checks the repository every minute","A larger build agent","An SNMP trap"],0,"A webhook sends an HTTP request to the pipeline the moment the push event happens. A cron job is still polling on a schedule.","Domain 5: webhooks and APIs"],["cp77",0,5,"A script must create a new resource through a provider's REST API. Which HTTP method is normally used to create it?",["GET","HEAD","DELETE","POST"],3,"POST is the standard method for creating a resource in REST APIs. GET and HEAD read without changing anything, and DELETE removes a resource.","Domain 5: APIs"],["cp78",0,5,"A bug appeared in production that never appeared in staging, because the image was rebuilt separately for each environment and pulled newer packages. What practice prevents this?",["Always deploy the 'latest' tag","Build once, tag it immutably, and promote the same image through each environment","Rebuild the image in every environment","Install the latest package versions when each container starts so that every environment is always current"],1,"Promoting one immutable build guarantees staging and production run identical artifacts. Rebuilding, 'latest' tags and install-at-start all let versions drift.","Domain 5: containers"],["cp79",0,5,"A pipeline fails with a parse error in its YAML definition, although every key name is spelled correctly. What is the most likely cause?",["Too many comments","Using double quotes around a string","Inconsistent indentation","The file uses lowercase keys"],2,"YAML uses indentation to define structure, so a misaligned line changes or breaks the hierarchy. Quotes, comments and lowercase keys are all valid.","Domain 5: data formats"],["cp80",0,6,"Users say an internal cloud application is slow. According to the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology, what should the administrator do first?",["Restart the application servers","Document the fix in the knowledge base","Implement a larger instance type","Identify the problem by gathering information, questioning users and checking recent changes"],3,"The first step is to identify the problem: gather details, find symptoms and review recent changes. Acting before knowing the cause can make things worse.","Domain 6: methodology"],["cp81",0,6,"A VM in a private subnet cannot reach an update repository on the internet. A NAT gateway exists in the public subnet. Security groups allow outbound traffic. What should be checked next?",["The load balancer listener's TLS certificate and the security policy attached to it","The VM's disk IOPS","The CDN cache settings","The private subnet's route table for a default route to the NAT gateway"],3,"Without a 0.0.0.0/0 route pointing at the NAT gateway, private traffic has no path out. Disk performance and certificates do not affect outbound routing.","Domain 6: network troubleshooting"],["cp82",0,6,"After a new database was deployed, the application tier gets connection timeouts on TCP 5432. The database is running and healthy. What is the most likely cause?",["The database's security group lacks an inbound rule for 5432 from the application tier","The database is using too much memory","The application uses the wrong HTTP method","The object storage bucket is private"],0,"A timeout with a healthy database usually means traffic is being dropped, and a missing inbound rule is the most common reason. Memory pressure would show errors or slowness, not dropped packets.","Domain 6: network troubleshooting"],["cp83",0,6,"New instances launched by an autoscaling group are marked unhealthy and replaced every few minutes, although the application works when tested directly on port 8080. What is the most likely cause?",["The instances are too large","The load balancer health check is probing the wrong port or path","The autoscaling group has a maximum size set lower than its desired capacity","The instances have too much memory"],1,"If the health check targets a port or path the app does not serve, healthy instances fail checks and get replaced in a loop. Instance size and a maximum group size do not cause health check failures.","Domain 6: deployment troubleshooting"],["cp84",0,6,"A nightly script that calls a cloud API thousands of times starts failing with HTTP 429 responses. What is the best fix?",["Retry immediately in a tight loop","Switch the API calls to plain HTTP","Add exponential backoff with jitter and reduce or batch requests","Grant the script's identity administrator rights so the API stops limiting its requests"],2,"HTTP 429 means Too Many Requests: the caller is being rate limited. Backoff and fewer calls respect the limit, while immediate retries make throttling worse and more permissions do not change rate limits.","Domain 6: performance troubleshooting"],["cp85",0,6,"A template deployment fails with an error that the regional vCPU limit for the instance family has been reached. What should the administrator do?",["Rewrite the template in another infrastructure-as-code language that the provider does not limit","Request a quota increase or use another region or instance family","Delete the virtual network and try again","Disable encryption on the volumes"],1,"Service quotas cap resources per region; raising the quota, freeing capacity or choosing a different region or family resolves the error. The IaC language and network are unrelated.","Domain 6: deployment troubleshooting"],["cp86",0,6,"A user's IAM policy allows reading an object storage bucket, but requests still return 403 Access Denied. What should the administrator check first?",["Whether the bucket uses the archive tier","Whether the bucket policy or an organization policy contains an explicit deny","The user's desktop screen resolution","Whether the bucket has versioning enabled"],1,"In cloud policy evaluation an explicit deny anywhere overrides any allow. Versioning and storage tier do not produce a 403 for an allowed user.","Domain 6: access troubleshooting"],["cp87",0,6,"A website was moved to a new load balancer and the DNS record updated, but some users reach the old servers for hours. What is the most likely reason?",["The TLS certificate on the new load balancer uses a different key size than the old certificate did","The new load balancer is too small","The old record's TTL was long, so resolvers are still using cached answers","The web servers need more memory"],2,"Resolvers cache records for the TTL, so a long TTL delays the change. Lowering the TTL well before a migration shortens this window.","Domain 6: network troubleshooting"],["cp88",0,6,"The cloud bill doubled this month. The cost report shows a large charge for storage volumes and public IP addresses that are not attached to any running VM. What is the cause?",["Too many IAM users","A security group misconfiguration that allowed too much traffic into the virtual network","Distributed tracing overhead","Orphaned resources left behind after VMs were deleted"],3,"Deleting a VM does not always delete its disks, snapshots or reserved IPs, and these keep billing. Security groups and IAM users are free.","Domain 6: cost troubleshooting"],["cp89",0,6,"Overnight, browsers start showing security warnings for a company's API, and client scripts fail with TLS handshake errors. No code changed. What should be checked first?",["The container image size","The API's CPU utilization","The database backup schedule and retention settings","The TLS certificate's expiration date"],3,"A certificate expiring with no other change produces exactly these symptoms. Automating renewal and monitoring expiry dates prevents it.","Domain 6: access troubleshooting"],["cp90",0,6,"A pipeline that deployed successfully for months now fails at the deploy step with an authentication error, and no code has changed. What is the most likely cause?",["The Git repository has too many branches","The YAML file needs more comments","The service principal or access token used by the pipeline has expired or was rotated","The build agent has too much disk space"],2,"Credentials used by automation often expire or are rotated, which breaks pipelines with no code change. Branch count and comments do not affect authentication.","Domain 6: automation troubleshooting"]]);
